  :doc/body "# ayatori Co-Scientist Iteration 08 - the unit changes, and the cursor removes the whole term

> Iterations 03-07 measured ROUND TRIPS, and 07 closed by saying ratios are the
> wrong unit and absolute trips the right one. For the pack question it was. A
> production measurement on 2026-09-10 says it is the wrong unit for the regime
> that is actually failing, and names a different one.

## P. What production measured, and why round trips cannot explain it

`datoms.kotobase.net` (net-kotobase/control-plane, root ADR-2609100100) burns
25-32s of CPU per read/fold/transact on `yoro-social-v2` and is killed at the
`cpu_ms` ceiling. Two probes were deployed into the live Worker to measure the
killed invocation itself -- both report DURING the run, because a killed
isolate never reaches its own summary:

    read     cpu=23838ms  handlerRuns:5 syncGets:10 (24ms)    gets:0  decrypts:200 (19552ms)
    read     cpu=25917ms  handlerRuns:5 syncGets:326 (4045ms) gets:0  decrypts:400 (22101ms)
    transact cpu=32000ms  handlerRuns:5 syncGets:10 (24ms)    gets:122 decrypts:78 (21835ms)
    fold     cpu=32000ms  handlerRuns<100                     gets:75 decrypts:1725 (1638ms)

Three candidate causes died against these numbers:

  - **I/O.** A 24-second read reports `gets:0`: every block was already cached.
    The fold's whole graph is 75 blocks.
  - **`with-blocks` restarts.** `handlerRuns` stays under 100 everywhere; the
    every-hundredth probe line never printed once.
  - **AES-GCM itself.** 1800 noble decrypts benchmark at 92ms (64B rows) to
    2.3s (64KB rows) locally, against a 30s budget.

**Round trips cannot explain a cost incurred when there are none.** What is
left is per-row work: CPU between store calls, at 50-100ms per decrypt
interval, on a graph of 75 blocks.

So this iteration counts ROWS DECRYPTED.

## Q. One snapshot, one query, two access paths

`bench/materialize_vs_cursor.cljk`. Both arms answer the same query over the
same snapshot bytes, so a difference is the access path and nothing else. The
result set is held FIXED at 5 subjects while the graph grows -- the point being
that the live path's cost grows anyway.

  A  `arrangement.core/restore` -> `source/materialized`. Its own docstring:
     \\\"re-asserting every recovered triple\\\". This is the shape the live path
     has; `xrpc.cljs` records the same thing from the other side, that
     `hot-datoms` \\\"re-decrypts every row on EVERY request\\\".
  B  `source/cursor-async` via `remote/q-async`. Its docstring: \\\"the snapshot
     is never hydrated into an in-memory db\\\".

       n   quads | materialize gets  decrypts | cursor gets  decrypts | rows  agree
     250     500 |               4       500  |          5       10   |    5  true
     500    1000 |               6      1000  |          7       10   |    5  true
    1000    2000 |               9      2000  |          7       10   |    5  true
    2000    4000 |              23      4000  |          8       10   |    5  true

**materialize decrypts == the quad count, exactly, at every size. The cursor
decrypts 10 at every size** -- two per result row, and the result size is what
was held fixed. `agree` is the gate: an arm that is faster and returns
different rows is not a faster arm.

Block reads move the same way but far less dramatically (23 vs 8 at n=2000),
which is the point of section P restated from the other side: the term that
matters here is not the one round trips measure.

## What this says about the production graph

`yoro-social-v2` is 75 blocks and decrypts ~1725 rows to answer a fold, and
~200-400 to answer a `limit 1` read. On this curve a cursor answers the same
`limit 1` in single digits. **The cursor removes the term, it does not shrink
it.**

## What it does NOT say -- three limits, stated rather than implied

1. **This is the SNAPSHOT half only.** A kotobase read is snapshot + novelty,
   and unfolded novelty still has to be applied on top. The cursor covers the
   snapshot. Whether the production 25s is mostly snapshot or mostly novelty is
   **still unsplit** -- the fold probe (`max_novelty=4`, decrypts ~1725) says
   the fold's bulk is snapshot, but a fold rebuilds the whole snapshot by
   definition, so a cursor cannot replace a fold. The read has not been split.
2. **The live datom plane does not use ayatori.** Its consumers are
   datomic-client-shim, org-graphql-http, org-opencypher-cypher and
   org-w3-sparql-protocol -- the surfaces root ADR-2608039975 records as not
   live. Adoption inside `kotobase-server`'s `hot-datoms` is a real
   integration, and root AGENTS.md names the two-implementation problem
   explicitly as the thing not to re-open.
3. **This bench uses identity crypto and an in-memory block map.** Production
   `decrypt-fn` is real AES-GCM and `blind-fn` real HMAC. The COUNT is the
   finding; the wall clock here is not a production figure. That is also why
   the count is the right unit: production's cost tracks the count, and the
   local control that made the identity substitution is exactly what produced
   the 10x gap noted in ADR-2609100100.

`cursor` proper is JVM-only. This bench drove `cursor-async` under nbb, so the
Worker-native path is the one that produced these numbers.

## Seeds for iteration 09

  - **Split the read.** Phase timers around cold-datoms vs novelty apply, in
    the live Worker, same during-the-run reporting. Until that exists, \\\"the
    cursor fixes the read\\\" is a projection from the snapshot half.
  - Sweep the result size, not just the graph size. The cursor's 10 is 2 per
    row here; the claim worth testing is that it stays 2 per row when the
    result is thousands, where a covering-index scan may stop being cheaper
    than one pass over everything. There is a crossover and nobody has found it.
  - The novelty side has no cursor at all. Whether one is possible over a cons
    chain of tx blocks -- or whether the answer there is simply to make fold
    reachable again -- is a design question this iteration did not touch."}]
